.TH "ELEMENT.GETATTRIBUTENODE" "3JS" "August 19, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.getAttributeNode \- Element: getAttributeNode() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]getAttributeNode()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]Element\f[R] interface returns the specified attribute of the
specified element, as an \f[CR]Attr\f[R] node.
It returns \f[CR]null\f[R] if the element doesn\(cqt have an attribute
with the given name.
.PP
This method is useful if you need the attribute\(cqs instance
properties.
If you only need the attribute\(cqs value, you can use the
\f[CR]getAttribute()\f[R] method instead.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
getAttributeNode(attrName)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]attrName\f[R]
A string specifying the name of the attribute.
When called on an HTML element in a DOM flagged as an HTML document, the
name is normalized to lowercase.
.SS Return value
An \f[CR]Attr\f[R] node for the attribute, or \f[CR]null\f[R] if the
element doesn\(cqt have an attribute with the given name.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] The \f[CR]Attr\f[R] node inherits from \f[CR]Node\f[R],
but is not considered a part of the document tree.
Common \f[CR]Node\f[R] attributes like \f[CR]parentNode\f[R],
\f[CR]previousSibling\f[R], and \f[CR]nextSibling\f[R] are
\f[CR]null\f[R] for an \f[CR]Attr\f[R] node.
You can, however, get the element to which the attribute belongs with
the \f[CR]ownerElement\f[R] property.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// html: <div id=\(dqtop\(dq />\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] t = document.getElementById(\(dqtop\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] idAttr = t.getAttributeNode(\(dqid\(dq);
console.log(idAttr.value); \f[I]// \(dqtop\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Document.createAttribute()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.setAttributeNode()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
